1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,010
This walkthrough covers the scraper changes between the June crawl and the October refresh.

2
00:00:07,010 --> 00:00:15,176
The goal is for you to understand what the code does and to check that the evidence supports each change.

3
00:00:15,176 --> 00:00:16,640
Two repos changed.

4
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:23,342
In scrape-website the scraper went from b4fb6ac, which the June crawl ran, to ffe35d6.

5
00:00:23,342 --> 00:00:29,890
In apo-mcp-server, the run settings went from the June code to release candidate 14.

6
00:00:29,890 --> 00:00:36,900
Every code panel in this deck is copied from git at those commits, with real line numbers.

7
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:44,258
The purpose of every change in this deck is the same: students’ answers should come from current, relevant pages.

8
00:00:44,258 --> 00:00:46,634
The scraper serves that in two ways.

9
00:00:46,634 --> 00:00:54,791
Less junk: fetch and keep only real pages, so error pages, duplicates, search results and past seminars never reach an answer.

10
00:00:54,791 --> 00:01:00,444
Faster: a full refresh in hours instead of days, so the content can be refreshed often.

11
00:01:00,444 --> 00:01:10,206
And one guardrail: every filter could drop a real page and every speed-up could get us blocked, so the deck also shows that nothing important was lost.

12
00:01:10,206 --> 00:01:15,088
What we ask of you is to read what each change does and check the evidence.

13
00:01:15,088 --> 00:01:22,410
Every change slide has four parts: what we saw, what changed, the code before and after, and what the run showed.

14
00:01:22,510 --> 00:01:24,005
Here is the baseline.

15
00:01:24,005 --> 00:01:31,195
The June 27 prod run reached only 30 of 81 sites; the other 51 refused it on the very first request.

16
00:01:31,195 --> 00:01:45,931
So the 23 largest sites were crawled from a laptop, one request at a time, from early June 27 to early June 29, about two days, restarted by hand four or five times because each site hit a two-hour timeout.

17
00:01:45,931 --> 00:01:51,341
What prod held afterwards, 156 thousand rows, was about sixty percent junk.

18
00:01:51,341 --> 00:02:03,230
In October, the same prod setup reached 90 of 91 sites during the refresh, in about nine hours, and the only miss, math, is blocked by Cloudflare and was run locally.

19
00:02:03,330 --> 00:02:05,413
Site by site, the same story.

20
00:02:05,413 --> 00:02:11,376
History took over 36 hours in June, start to finish, and visited 28 thousand URLs.

21
00:02:11,376 --> 00:02:16,333
In October it took 26 minutes and saved about 2,600 pages and files.

22
00:02:16,333 --> 00:02:27,252
Economics went from over 26 hours to 39 minutes; chemistry from nineteen and a half hours to 25 minutes; seas from nine and a half hours to 50 minutes.

23
00:02:27,252 --> 00:02:37,310
The June times include the gaps between restarts, so they overstate active crawling, but they are the real time it took to get the content.

24
00:02:37,310 --> 00:02:38,962
Two things drive this.

25
00:02:38,962 --> 00:02:41,979
Fewer URLs is the junk work in section A.

26
00:02:41,979 --> 00:02:48,660
Minutes instead of hours comes from less to fetch, plus more requests at once, in section B.

27
00:02:48,760 --> 00:02:52,015
Before the detail, the shape of the change.

28
00:02:52,015 --> 00:03:04,353
In scrape-website there are no new source files: every change is in one file, scraper dot py, which grew from about 1,100 lines in June to about 1,900 in October.

29
00:03:04,353 --> 00:03:08,062
Some of that arrived in July, in prod version 1.

30
00:03:08,062 --> 00:03:08,214
0.

31
00:03:08,214 --> 00:03:14,345
1: the fetch tiers that retry as Chrome and as a real browser, and retry backoff.

32
00:03:14,345 --> 00:03:18,205
Everything else in this deck is the October round.

33
00:03:18,205 --> 00:03:20,476
There are ten new test files.

34
00:03:20,476 --> 00:03:26,456
In apo-mcp-server, five existing files were edited, with three new test files.

35
00:03:26,456 --> 00:03:32,890
The same release also carries course-catalog work, which is not part of this review.

36
00:03:32,990 --> 00:03:36,402
Before the code, a picture of what a crawl does.

37
00:03:36,402 --> 00:03:41,306
In June, each site had one starting page, the url in sites dot toml.

38
00:03:41,306 --> 00:03:44,576
For classics that was the undergraduate page.

39
00:03:44,576 --> 00:03:55,237
The crawler opened it, collected its links, queued them in state dot db with the page they were found on, and went down the page, one link at a time.

40
00:03:55,237 --> 00:04:04,335
That is how it reached junk: search result pages, the same page again over http, and six citation-export files per publication.

41
00:04:04,335 --> 00:04:07,889
And a page that nothing links to was never found.

42
00:04:07,889 --> 00:04:13,504
Classics took eight hours fifteen minutes and visited over four thousand URLs.

43
00:04:13,504 --> 00:04:21,820
In October, the crawler starts from the site’s own list, its sitemap: 577 pages, each with the date it last changed.

44
00:04:21,820 --> 00:04:29,425
The exclude rules run on that list before anything is fetched, and all 577 go into the queue at the start.

45
00:04:29,425 --> 00:04:31,274
Links are still followed.

46
00:04:31,274 --> 00:04:36,320
Classics took 21 minutes, one request at a time, and visited 673 URLs.

47
00:04:36,420 --> 00:04:38,143
Here is the code for that.

48
00:04:38,143 --> 00:04:43,112
In June, scrape dot py passed use sitemap false, and no site turned it on.

49
00:04:43,112 --> 00:04:54,441
The old discovery used a plain client, tried two fixed addresses, and on any failure returned an empty list, so a site that refused it looked like a site with no sitemap.

50
00:04:54,441 --> 00:04:56,561
In October the default is true.

51
00:04:56,561 --> 00:05:02,126
Discovery reads robots dot txt first, and fetches through the same tiers as a page.

52
00:05:02,126 --> 00:05:09,414
Every entry is seeded or counted under a reason, like dropped excluded, and one log line per site reports it.

53
00:05:09,414 --> 00:05:22,400
Across the October run: fifty-four thousand seeds from eighty-nine thousand sitemap entries on seventy-eight sites, and seventeen and a half thousand junk addresses dropped before a single fetch.

54
00:05:22,500 --> 00:05:24,965
Some responses are not pages at all.

55
00:05:24,965 --> 00:05:31,677
In a test run, classics fetched almost 2,500 pages, and nearly 1,900 of them were page not found.

56
00:05:31,677 --> 00:05:41,881
Its links pages redirect to other websites, and the scraper read those sites’ links as if they were classics pages, inventing hundreds of addresses.

57
00:05:41,881 --> 00:05:48,181
In June, process url saved whatever came back, as long as it was not an access-denied page.

58
00:05:48,181 --> 00:05:58,523
Now there are three checks after the fetch: did the redirect leave the site, did the server send an image, video or audio, and is the status an error.

59
00:05:58,523 --> 00:06:00,988
None of those is saved or followed.

60
00:06:00,988 --> 00:06:09,480
Classics dropped from 65 minutes to 21, and across the refresh almost six thousand off-site and error pages were not saved.

61
00:06:09,580 --> 00:06:13,709
The exclude rules are a list of URL patterns the crawler skips.

62
00:06:13,709 --> 00:06:18,232
In June the list had eight blog patterns, like tag and author pages.

63
00:06:18,232 --> 00:06:20,854
Measurements showed what else was junk.

64
00:06:20,854 --> 00:06:28,391
On chemistry, filter links, one per combination of directory filters, were about 2,300 of the first 5,300 fetches.

65
00:06:28,391 --> 00:06:34,028
Publication pages offered six citation-export formats each, which ingest never reads.

66
00:06:34,028 --> 00:06:37,567
Search pages and calendar files are not pages either.

67
00:06:37,567 --> 00:06:44,581
Each new pattern carries a comment with the measurement behind it, so you can check the reason, not guess.

68
00:06:44,581 --> 00:06:55,330
The rules apply both when links are found and when the sitemap is read, and in October they dropped seventeen and a half thousand sitemap entries before any fetch.

69
00:06:55,430 --> 00:06:58,633
Event pages were the biggest single kind of junk.

70
00:06:58,633 --> 00:07:09,813
Seventy-four percent of the economics sitemap was one page per past seminar, going back years, and a student asking what is coming up could get an answer from an old one.

71
00:07:09,813 --> 00:07:12,428
In June there was no event rule at all.

72
00:07:12,428 --> 00:07:25,568
Now event pages are excluded by default, with three exceptions: deadline pages, four student-facing sites whose event pages are all this year, and any event the sitemap dates on or after June 1, 2026.

73
00:07:25,568 --> 00:07:29,687
That date lives in scrape dot py and needs moving each summer.

74
00:07:29,687 --> 00:07:34,525
The keep list came from Splunk: which event pages answers actually cited.

75
00:07:34,525 --> 00:07:42,370
Economics went from over six thousand sitemap entries to under two thousand seeds, and 270 of those are current events.

76
00:07:42,470 --> 00:07:43,884
Junk also means stale.

77
00:07:43,884 --> 00:07:50,507
Pages deleted from department sites since June were still in the knowledge base, still marked current.

78
00:07:50,507 --> 00:07:56,036
After a fresh scrape, every stored page the new crawl did not find is marked removed.

79
00:07:56,036 --> 00:08:03,494
In June that took two gateway calls per page: read the file’s attributes, then write them back with the new status.

80
00:08:03,494 --> 00:08:12,817
Measured in dev, an update replaces the whole attribute set, so the fast path rebuilds every attribute from the Postgres row and sends one call.

81
00:08:12,817 --> 00:08:23,425
During the refresh, 92 thousand rows were retired with no failures; economics later had two failures out of 23 thousand, on June files that never finished indexing.

82
00:08:23,425 --> 00:08:27,540
Career services’ June event pages now come back marked removed.

83
00:08:27,640 --> 00:08:28,332
Now speed.

84
00:08:28,332 --> 00:08:36,982
In June, seas was crawled one request at a time, about 40 pages a minute, and its scrape was estimated at almost five hours.

85
00:08:36,982 --> 00:08:45,564
The number of requests at once came only from sites dot toml, so changing it meant a code change and a new container image.

86
00:08:45,564 --> 00:08:55,321
Now scrape and ingest takes a concurrency flag and a delay flag, passes them to scrape dot py, and scrape dot py prefers them over the file.

87
00:08:55,321 --> 00:09:02,241
Measured on seas: about 40 pages a minute at one, 97 at two, and 187 at three, with nothing denied.

88
00:09:02,241 --> 00:09:07,570
The full seas scrape took 50 minutes; in June it took nine and a half hours.

89
00:09:07,670 --> 00:09:14,544
Not all of the speed came from code, and you should know that so you do not look for it in the scraper.

90
00:09:14,544 --> 00:09:23,888
When eight sites ran together in one task, raising the requests per site did nothing: each site still managed only 20 to 75 pages a minute.

91
00:09:23,888 --> 00:09:26,691
The task’s two CPUs were completely busy.

92
00:09:26,691 --> 00:09:31,163
So the run gave each task four CPUs and sixteen gigabytes instead.

93
00:09:31,163 --> 00:09:42,242
Combined throughput went up three to four times: task C went from about 280 pages a minute to over 1,200, and individual sites then finished in five to nine minutes.

94
00:09:42,242 --> 00:09:46,180
This is a run setting, recorded in the run order document.

95
00:09:46,280 --> 00:09:49,146
Going faster has a risk: sites push back.

96
00:09:49,146 --> 00:09:57,255
The sixteen College WordPress sites limit how many requests one client can make, and answer 429, too many requests.

97
00:09:57,255 --> 00:10:07,041
Every fetch method got that answer, even a real browser, and the page was counted as lost: ten to forty-five percent of those sites’ pages.

98
00:10:07,041 --> 00:10:11,026
In June, fetch with retry had no answer for this at all.

99
00:10:11,026 --> 00:10:16,338
Now, when every tier says 429, the scraper reads it as a rate, not a block.

100
00:10:16,338 --> 00:10:25,565
It pauses that whole site for fifteen seconds, then thirty, then sixty, retrying after each, and only then counts the page as lost.

101
00:10:25,565 --> 00:10:29,480
The WordPress group then lost zero pages on every site.

102
00:10:29,580 --> 00:10:33,519
The fetch tiers sit behind both of the last two slides.

103
00:10:33,519 --> 00:10:41,613
Sites behind Akamai refuse a plain client, and in June, prod was refused by 51 of 81 sites on its first request.

104
00:10:41,613 --> 00:10:49,706
The June report put that down to the network address; in October the same prod network reached all but one site.

105
00:10:49,706 --> 00:10:53,932
In June the scraper had one way to fetch: a plain request.

106
00:10:53,932 --> 00:10:55,723
In July, prod version 1.

107
00:10:55,723 --> 00:10:55,866
0.

108
00:10:55,866 --> 00:11:00,808
1 added tiers: on a 403 it retries as Chrome, then as a real browser.

109
00:11:00,808 --> 00:11:12,411
October adds one rule: after five refusals in a row, start with the Chrome tier for the rest of that site, which skips a request we already know will be refused.

110
00:11:12,411 --> 00:11:16,780
That rule has no separate measurement, and we should say so.

111
00:11:16,880 --> 00:11:23,761
The last speed piece is a procedure, not code, and reviewers will otherwise look for it in the scraper.

112
00:11:23,761 --> 00:11:29,105
Sites run in groups by hosting platform, each group with settings that suit it.

113
00:11:29,105 --> 00:11:32,311
The four largest sites each get their own task.

114
00:11:32,311 --> 00:11:37,989
The Akamai Drupal sites run eight to a task at five to seven requests, on four CPUs.

115
00:11:37,989 --> 00:11:39,593
Pantheon runs at three.

116
00:11:39,593 --> 00:11:45,605
The WordPress sites run three at a time, one request each, because they limit per client.

117
00:11:45,605 --> 00:11:52,753
Two rules hold it together: no site is in two groups, and upload workers across all tasks stay near sixty.

118
00:11:52,753 --> 00:11:56,961
At about 120, the gateway refused about five thousand uploads.

119
00:11:56,961 --> 00:11:59,700
The procedure lives in RUN ORDER dot md.

120
00:11:59,800 --> 00:12:00,794
The guardrail.

121
00:12:00,794 --> 00:12:07,821
Every filter can drop a real page, and every speed-up can get us blocked, so did we lose anything?

122
00:12:07,821 --> 00:12:17,120
Out of 79 thousand URLs visited during the refresh, 267 were denied, and most of those are unpublished pages that refuse everyone.

123
00:12:17,120 --> 00:12:24,076
Every site with a sitemap logs one line that accounts for every entry, seeded or dropped and why.

124
00:12:24,076 --> 00:12:28,406
The WordPress sites lost no pages once the 429 pause was in.

125
00:12:28,406 --> 00:12:33,659
There are known gaps, and showing them is what makes the rest believable.

126
00:12:33,659 --> 00:12:37,918
Math is blocked by Cloudflare from AWS and was run locally.

127
00:12:37,918 --> 00:12:44,448
282 PowerPoint and old Word files could not be converted, which release candidate 13 fixes.

128
00:12:44,448 --> 00:12:50,340
And 5,367 uploads refused by the overloaded gateway were sent in a follow-up pass.

129
00:12:50,440 --> 00:13:05,252
Each behavior in sections A and B has tests that pin it: the 429 pause, the sticky Chrome tier, the browser cap, off-site and error pages, media, the exclude rules with the event keep list, and sitemap discovery.

130
00:13:05,252 --> 00:13:12,938
All 80 scraper tests pass at the October commit, and the 34 orchestration tests pass at release candidate 14.

131
00:13:12,938 --> 00:13:22,580
What the tests do not cover is live sites and real speed: they use simulated fetches, so the run figures are the only evidence for those.

132
00:13:22,680 --> 00:13:26,429
Four things a reviewer should know before approving.

133
00:13:26,429 --> 00:13:31,115
First, prod runs code from a pull request that is still a draft.

134
00:13:31,115 --> 00:13:36,234
Second, the June 1 event cutoff needs an owner to move it each summer.

135
00:13:36,234 --> 00:13:50,725
Third, the 429 pause applies to one site, but the WordPress platform counts requests per client address, so sites sharing a task do not share the pause; today the run recipe protects us, not the code.

136
00:13:50,725 --> 00:13:58,439
Fourth, a sitemap cut off at fifty thousand entries is logged but not flagged to the rest of the pipeline.

137
00:13:58,439 --> 00:14:02,620
None of these blocks the changes; they are what to watch.
